          ],
          "content": "MATHEMATICS"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.138,
            0.778,
            0.159
          ],
          "content": "Substituting the values of $a , \\Sigma f d _ { i }$ and $\\Sigma f _ { i }$ from Table 13.4, we get"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.305,
            0.171,
            0.666,
            0.212
          ],
          "content": "\\left[ \\overline{x} \\right. = \\left. 47.5 + \\frac{435}{30} \\right] = 47.5 + 14.5 = 62 .",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.221,
            0.716,
            0.241
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, the mean of the marks obtained by the students is $6 2$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.246,
            0.815,
            0.266
          ],
          "content": "The method discussed above is called the **Assumed Mean Method**."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.278,
            0.901,
            0.337
          ],
          "content": "**Activity 1 :** From the Table 13.3 find the mean by taking each of $x _ { i }$ (i.e., 17.5, 32.5, and so on) as $^ { \\circ } a ^ { \\prime }$ . What do you observe? You will find that the mean determined in each case is the same, i.e., 62. (Why ?)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.343,
            0.901,
            0.381
          ],
          "content": "So, we can say that the value of the mean obtained does not depend on the choice of $^ { \\circ } a ^ { \\prime }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.386,
            0.902,
            0.447
          ],
          "content": "Observe that in Table 13.4, the values in Column 4 are all multiples of 15. So, if we divide the values in the entire Column 4 by 15, we would get smaller numbers to multiply with $f _ { i ^ { * } }$ (Here, 15 is the class size of each class interval.)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.461,
            0.844,
            0.5
          ],
          "content": "So, let $u _ { _ i } = \\frac { x _ { _ i } - a } { h }$ , where a is the assumed mean and h is the class size."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.508,
            0.901,
            0.549
          ],
          "content": "Now, we calculate $u _ { _ i }$ in this way and continue as before (i.e., find $f _ { i }   u _ { i }$ and then $\\Sigma f _ { i } u _ { i } )$ . Taking $h = 1 5 ^ { \\circ }$ , let us form Table 13.5."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.101,
            0.58,
            0.896,
            0.809
          ],
          "content": "| Class interval | f<sub>i</sub> | x<sub>i</sub> | d<sub>i</sub> = x<sub>i</sub> - a | xi - a u<sub>i</sub> = h | f<sub>i</sub>u<sub>i</sub> |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 10 - 25 | 2 | 17.5 | -30 | -2 | -4 |\n| 25 - 40 | 3 | 32.5 | -15 | -1 | -3 |\n| 40 - 55 | 7 | 47.5 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| 55 - 70 | 6 | 62.5 | 15 | 1 | 6 |\n| 70 - 85 | 6 | 77.5 | 30 | 2 | 12 |\n| 85 - 100 | 6 | 92.5 | 45 | 3 | 18 |\n| Total | Σf<sub>i</sub> = 30 |  |  |  | Σf<sub>i</sub>u<sub>i</sub> = 29 |",
          "captions": [
            {
